(1) Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 3O covers children in conflict with the law and child victims of criminal acts, and is the obligation and responsibility of the government and society.

(2) Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through: a. treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of the child;

b. provision of special child companion officers from an early stage; c. provision of special facilities and infrastructure;

d. imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of the child;

e. continuous monitoring and recording of the development of children in conflict with the law;

f. provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; and

g. protection from disclosure of identity through the mass media and to avoid labeling.

(3) Special protection for children who become victims of criminal acts as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through: a. rehabilitation efforts, both in institutions and outside institutions; b. efforts to protect from disclosure of identity through the mass media and to avoid labeling;

c. provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses and expert witnesses, both physical, mental, and social; and

d. provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments.

Article 27

(l) The government in providing maintenance and care is obliged to endeavor and assist children, so that children can:

a. participate;
b. freely express opinions and think in accordance with their conscience and religion;
c. freely receive oral or written information in accordance with the age stage and development of the child;
d. freely associate and assemble;
e. freely rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts; and
f. obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements.
(2) The efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are adjusted to the age, developed and the level of ability of the child, and the environment so as not to hinder and disrupt the child's development.

Article 11

(1) The form of Integrated Services for victims organized by YXIP2A includes:

1t

a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;

b. medicolegal services constitute a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;

c. psychosocial services constitute services provided by companions in the context of traumatic recovery of the victim's condition, including provision of space and victim's home to protect victims from threats and intimidation to victims;

d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process;

e. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.

(2) The service mechanism as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented based on Standard Operating Procedures.

